  "doc_id": "d-20261124-212917-2cf593",
  "title": "qubit calculations",
  "added_date": "2026-11-24 21:29:17",
  "last_updated": "2026-11-24 22:21:34",
  "draft": false,
  "summary": "A qubit (quantum bit) performs a calculation by exploiting the rules of quantum mechanics — specifically superposition, interference, and entanglement — to explore and manipulate many possible states simultaneously.",
  "summary_html": "<p>A qubit (quantum bit) performs a calculation by exploiting the rules of quantum mechanics — specifically superposition, interference, and entanglement — to explore and manipulate many possible states simultaneously.</p>",
  "content_html": "<h1>qubit calculations</h1>\n<div class=\"docsViewer__summary\"><p>A qubit (quantum bit) performs a calculation by exploiting the rules of quantum mechanics — specifically superposition, interference, and entanglement — to explore and manipulate many possible states simultaneously.</p></div>\n<p>A <strong>classical bit</strong> can be either <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span> or <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span>, but a <strong>qubit</strong> can be in a <strong>superposition</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>where</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>β</mi></mrow></math></span> are <strong>complex amplitudes</strong> (not probabilities directly).</li>\n<li><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>α</mi><msup><mo>|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>β</mi><msup><mo>|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When you <em>measure</em> the qubit, it “collapses” to <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>α</mi><msup><mo>|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span> or to <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>β</mi><msup><mo>|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n<h2>How a qubit performs a calculation</h2>\n<p>The magic lies in how <em>quantum gates</em> transform these amplitudes.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Initialization</strong> – Start in a known state (say <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span>).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Superposition</strong> – Apply a <em>Hadamard gate</em> <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span>:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>Now the qubit is simultaneously <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> <em>and</em> <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Quantum operations</strong> – Apply unitary gates (e.g., phase shifts, rotations, controlled gates) that evolve the amplitudes:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>′</mi></msup><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>These operations are linear and reversible, and they allow the qubit to <strong>encode relationships</strong> between possible states.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Interference</strong> – The amplitudes can <strong>add or cancel</strong> like waves, amplifying correct results and suppressing incorrect ones.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Measurement</strong> – Collapsing the state gives one result, but if the algorithm was designed properly, it will yield the <em>right answer with high probability</em>.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Simplified view of Quantum Parallelism</h2>\n<p>Suppose you want to evaluate a function <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> for both <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>. A classical computer must compute <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> sequentially or in parallel hardware. A quantum computer prepares:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>and then applies a quantum gate that encodes <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> in the <strong>phase</strong> or <strong>amplitude</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>⟩</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>Now both evaluations coexist in superposition.</p>\n<p>Later interference is used to extract global properties of <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> (e.g., whether it’s balanced or constant — as in <em>Deutsch’s algorithm</em>).</p>\n<h2>Scaling up — Multiple qubits and entanglement</h2>\n<p>When you have <strong><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math></span> qubits</strong>, the state space has <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> amplitudes:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>Ψ</mi><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>A single operation (unitary transformation) can manipulate all <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math></span> amplitudes <em>at once</em>.\nThat exponential “parallelism” is what gives quantum computers their potential power — though it must be carefully directed by interference and entanglement to be useful.</p>\n<h2>Example algorithms</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Grover’s algorithm</strong>: uses interference to find one marked item in an unsorted database in <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> steps (vs. <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> classically).</li>\n<li><strong>Shor’s algorithm</strong>: uses quantum Fourier transforms to find periodicity, allowing efficient factoring.</li>\n<li><strong>Quantum simulations</strong>: use qubits to directly represent the state of quantum systems — the most natural use.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Summary analogy</h2>\n<p>Think of a qubit as a <strong>tiny wave</strong> rather than a point.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Quantum gates <strong>rotate and interfere</strong> these waves.</li>\n<li>The calculation is the <em>dance of amplitudes</em>.</li>\n<li>Measurement samples the <strong>pattern that remains</strong> — the interference pattern that encodes your answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Bloch Sphere: A qubit as a point on a sphere</h2>\n<p>The <em>Bloch sphere</em> is the most intuitive way to <em>see</em> how a qubit performs a calculation.</p>\n<figure class=\"docsViewerFigure docsViewerFigure--full-column docsViewerFigure--natural-width\"><img src=\"docs-media:collections/library/svg/d-20261124-212917-2cf593/bloch-sphere.svg\" alt=\"Bloch sphere with ket zero and ket one at the poles and the plus state on the positive x-axis. The state vector makes an angle theta with positive z and projects onto the equator at azimuth phi.\" title=\"Bloch sphere with ket zero and ket one at the poles and the plus state on the positive x-axis. The state vector makes an angle theta with positive z and projects onto the equator at azimuth phi.\"><figcaption><span class=\"docsViewerFigure__caption\">Qubit states on the Bloch sphere</span><span class=\"docsViewerFigure__summary\">The polar angle <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></math></span> starts at <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math></span>. The azimuth <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></math></span> runs from <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> toward <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span> in the equatorial plane. Applying <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span> gives <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span> on <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span>.</span></figcaption></figure>\n<p>A qubit’s state</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>can be rewritten (up to a global phase) as</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>cos</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>sin</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>This corresponds to a point on the surface of a unit sphere with angles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></math></span></strong> → polar angle from the north pole (controls the ratio between <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span>).</li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></math></span></strong> → azimuth measured from <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> toward <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span> in the equatorial plane (gives the relative phase).</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State</th>\n<th><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></math></span></th>\n<th><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></math></span></th>\n<th>Position on Bloch sphere</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>North pole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>South pole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> on the equator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>−</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span> on the equator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span> on the equator</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Quantum gates as rotations</h2>\n<p>Each single-qubit unitary gate acts as a rotation of the Bloch vector:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Gate</th>\n<th>Rotation</th>\n<th>Example effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>X (NOT)</strong></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span> around the <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span>-axis</td>\n<td>Flips the basis states</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Y</strong></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span> around the <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></span>-axis</td>\n<td>Flips the basis states with a phase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Z</strong></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span> around the <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math></span>-axis</td>\n<td>Changes phase only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hadamard (H)</strong></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span> around the diagonal <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td>Creates equal superposition from a basis state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phase (S)</strong></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></math></span> around the <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math></span>-axis</td>\n<td>Adds phase <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> to <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>So, performing a <strong>quantum calculation</strong> is equivalent to moving the qubit around this sphere in a controlled way — each gate rotates the vector; interference arises from how these rotations combine.</p>\n<h2>Measurement as projection</h2>\n<p>A measurement in the computational basis returns <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span> or <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span> and leaves the qubit at the corresponding pole:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Closer to north → higher chance of 0</li>\n<li>Closer to south → higher chance of 1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Mathematically:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfrac><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mfrac><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>You can think of the qubit as a tiny compass needle pointing somewhere on this sphere. Every gate you apply nudges or spins it; the direction encodes amplitude and phase. When you measure, you’re asking, “Is it pointing mostly north or south?” Thus, a quantum calculation = precise control of rotations on the Bloch sphere.</p>\n<h2>Deutsch algorithm circuit</h2>\n<p>The <em>Deutsch algorithm circuit</em> is the simplest quantum algorithm that demonstrates real computational advantage over classical methods. You have a black box (called an <em>oracle</em>) that computes a function <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>→</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math></span>, and you must determine if <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math></span> is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Constant</strong> → <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Balanced</strong> → <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≠</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A classical computer must check both <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> <strong>and</strong> <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span>.<br />\nA <strong>quantum computer</strong> can determine which it is with <em>one</em> evaluation.</p>\n<p>There are two qubits:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Qubit</th>\n<th>Initial state</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Top</td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td>Represents the input <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bottom</td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td>Provides a workspace for <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"docsViewerFigure docsViewerFigure--full-column\"><img src=\"docs-media:collections/library/svg/d-20261124-212917-2cf593/deutsch-circuit.svg\" alt=\"Deutsch circuit with two input wires. Apply a Hadamard to both qubits, apply one oracle, then apply a Hadamard and measure the top qubit.\" title=\"Deutsch circuit with two input wires. Apply a Hadamard to both qubits, apply one oracle, then apply a Hadamard and measure the top qubit.\"></figure>\n<h3>Step 1 — Initialization</h3>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<h3>Step 2 — Apply Hadamard gates (H) to both qubits</h3>\n<p>Hadamard puts them into superpositions:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>⟩</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mo>|</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⊗</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>−</mo><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>Now the first qubit is both 0 and 1 — it’s ready to evaluate <em>both inputs at once</em>.</p>\n<h3>Step 3 — Oracle (<span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></math></span>) acts</h3>\n<p>The oracle encodes <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> by flipping the second qubit if <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span>:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>Applied to our superposition, it “tags” each component with a phase <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span>:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>⊗</mo><mo>|</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>→ the second qubit factors out (it’s no longer needed)<br />\n→ the first qubit now <em>encodes the parity of the function</em> in its <strong>phase</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Step 4 — Final Hadamard and measurement</h3>\n<p>Applying <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math></span> again to the first qubit creates <em>interference</em>. The joint state before measurement is:</p>\n<div class=\"math block\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>⟩</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⊕</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>⟩</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mo>|</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math>\n</div>\n<p>The factor <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math></span> is a global phase. Measuring the top qubit gives the following results:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math></span></th>\n<th>Top qubit</th>\n<th>Measurement outcome</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Constant (<span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span>)</td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Balanced (<span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≠</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span>)</td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>|</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>⟩</mi></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Thus, one measurement of the first qubit gives the answer with 100% certainty — after only <em>one</em> oracle call.</p>\n<p>The algorithm uses the <em>wave nature of probability amplitudes</em>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Hadamards create superpositions.</li>\n<li>The oracle encodes <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> as phase shifts.</li>\n<li>The final Hadamard causes interference: constructive if constant, destructive if balanced.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So rather than evaluating two cases separately, the algorithm compares both possibilities through interference — something classical computation can’t do directly.</p>\n<p>This tiny circuit is the foundation of larger quantum algorithms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Deutsch–Jozsa</em> generalizes it to n bits.</li>\n<li><em>Grover’s</em> and <em>Shor’s</em> algorithms rely on similar interference patterns.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It’s the first example of <strong>quantum parallelism</strong>: evaluating many paths at once and collapsing them through interference to reveal a global property.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Quantum concept</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Initialize</td>\n<td>Classical setup</td>\n<td>Start at known state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Hadamard gates</td>\n<td>Superposition</td>\n<td>Test all inputs simultaneously</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Oracle <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></math></span></td>\n<td>Phase kickback</td>\n<td>Encode <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span> in relative phase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Hadamard</td>\n<td>Interference</td>\n<td>Reveal parity of <span class=\"math inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n"
}
